VST3: receive MIDI program changes (sq8l-revival/SQ8L#40), and name the programs
the host lists (sq8l-revival/SQ8L#65).

VST3 has no raw MIDI program change event, and DPF's own TODO list says as much
("== MIDI / - MIDI program changes"). The host converts the message into a change
on the parameter flagged kIsProgramChange, and only routes it at all if the plugin
describes a program list through IUnitInfo - which DPF stubs out
("{v3_unit_info|NOT}"). Its program parameter also had nowhere to go: in process()
any internal parameter that is not a MIDI CC hits `continue`.

So a VST3 plugin built with DPF could not see a program change at all, while every
other format receives it as the two bytes it is.

- dpf_unit_info implements IUnitInfo with one root unit owning one program list of
  the plugin's program count, its entries named by getProgramName() - the same names
  the program parameter converts its value to, which is where a host that reads no
  unit info takes its list from. Names are per instance, so this is one object per
  instance, owned by the component and by the controller like dpf_audio_processor.
  get_unit_by_bus maps every channel of the event bus to that unit, which is what
  lets an omni plugin be reached on any channel; left unimplemented, hosts route
  only channel 1, or nothing.
- Both unref paths count it among the child interfaces that keep a component or a
  controller from being deleted. A host holds the unit info for as long as it holds
  the plugin and releases the component first - REAPER does - and since the object
  lives inside the component, deleting it there hands the host a pointer into freed
  memory: it crashed on removing the plugin, every time. Unlisted children are
  exactly what that "unclean instances" chain is for.
- The program parameter now names that unit (unit_id 0) instead of keeping
  whatever the caller passed in, and is queryable on the component as well as the
  controller, since hosts differ on which they ask.
- process() turns a change on it into a MIDI program change event, so the plugin
  sees the same two bytes it gets from VST2, CLAP, AU and LV2 and needs no
  format-specific path. Only values a program change could have produced (0..127)
  are converted; above that the parameter behaves exactly as it did.

Event order on one sample is the subtle part, placeSorted appending equal offsets in
insertion order. Two things follow.

Program changes need a pass of their own rather than a branch in the CC loop,
because the order a host lists its parameter queues in is its own business - by
parameter id, in practice. The program parameter's id is below
kVst3InternalParameterMidiCC_start, so one pass delivers the program change ahead
of the bank select sent with it on the same timestamp, and since bank select is
defined as a prefix to the program change, the program would load from the previous
bank.

Note events are now collected last instead of first. Sequencers emit a tick's
controllers, program change and bend ahead of its notes, because a note at that
tick is meant to sound with the patch and the controller values the tick sets up;
MIDI being a byte stream, it has no same-tick rule of its own to appeal to, and the
other four formats simply inherit the host's serial order. Collected first, as DPF
had it, a program change landed after the note it was meant to precede and the note
sounded with the previous program - and a CC landed after a note that should have
read it. The cost is that a block of over kMaxMidiEvents events now drops notes
rather than controllers; at 512 per block that needs a pathological stream, and the
alternative is worse in every ordinary one.

